@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        No theologian of sound judgment from the Reformed Church wishes to deny a general 
        intention or ordination concerning the salvation of each and every person by the death 
        of Christ, on this condition: if he will believe.
    </span>
    <span>John Davenant, Response to the French Reformed Churches</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        Through His life, ministry, and obedience of the law, Christ became the perfect sacrifice to atone for
        the sins of humanity, and He also became the perfect high priest to offer that sacrifice to the Father.
    </li>
    <li>
        Christ was pierced for the transgressions of sinners on the cross, and his death paid the debt that was
        due for sin. He was punished in place of sinners, and in doing so defeated sin, death, 
        the devil, and the powers of Hell.
    </li>
    <li>
        Christ's atonement is infinite in worth and value, more than covering the price to redeem all men.
    </li>
    <li>
        Through Christ's atonement, God establishes the well-meant offer of the gospel: That all who have faith
        shall not perish but have eternal life. The forgiveness of sins is available and offered to all, but it is
        received and applied by faith alone.
    </li>
    <li>
        Through the atonement, God effectually secures the forgiveness of sins for those whom he predestined to
        salvation in Christ.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />